{"id": "27791", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=27791", "title": "Plain of Jars", "text": "The Plain of Jars is a UNESCO world heritage site in Central Laos. It consists of thousands of stone jars scattered around the valleys and foothills of the Xiangkhoang Plateau.\nUnderstand.\nThe Plain of Jars combines ancient and contemporary history with insights into Phuan, Hmong, Khmu and Tai Dam culture. Nature encounters are easily accessible off the beaten track.\nUnlike ancient Greece or the First Emperor of China that date from around the same time, very little is known about the civilisation that created the Plain of Jars. Excavations have uncovered bones and pots of cremated human remains around the jars. There is a cave at Site 1 that was used for cremations. It is theorized that the jars were used to store bodies for 1\u20132 years before the bones were cremated and buried.\nIn the 1960s and 1970s, the area suffered badly due to heavy bombardment by Americans during the Vietnam War. The locals have found imaginative ways to rebuild their lives and surroundings, sometimes using the remnants as part of their daily life; bomb fragments became spoons, and a distinct Xiengkhouang-style of architecture has been created, incorporating bombshells as building material and d\u00e9cor for houses.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is in Phonsavan. Lao Airlines offers six flights a week in peak season and four flights in low season to Vientiane.\nBy bus.\nIf you are travelling to Phonsavan from Vientiane, you can take either VIP buses or local buses. The buses leave from the northern bus terminal and take about 10\u201312 hours. The roads are paved, but there are plenty of bends. The bus trip from Vang Vieng takes 7\u20138 hours. Minibus run daily from Luang Prabang via Road 13 and Road 7 and takes 11 hours for the 260-km-long ride, due to extremely poor road conditions. You could also hire a minivan in either Luang Prabang or Vientiane.\nComing from Vinh or Hanoi in Vietnam, visas are available on arrival at the Nam Ka border crossing, which is open daily from 06:00\u201318:00. The bus from Vinh leaves four days a week and takes 12 hours. There is one per week from Hanoi.\nGet around.\nTo get to other towns in the province, you can take local buses or pick-up trucks. Travel agents in Phonsavan arrange bike, motorbike and car rentals. A bike costs, depending on quality, from 20,000 kip per day. You can rent scooters for about 100,000 kip/day (70,000 kip in low season). The roads to the Site 1 are paved or in very good condition. Renting a minivan costs about US$50-80, and a four-wheel-drive costs over US$100. Depending on the company, the cost either includes or excludes petrol. All prices greatly vary depending on season and availability.\nMotorbikes are available for rent. All of them are semi-automatic, so you do not have to be an experienced biker. You can visit all three jar sites, but sites 2 and 3 can get a little tricky if it rains. Some places, such as War Spoon Village, require an expensive tour guide. Even if it is a planned move by the tour companies, it is still fun to ride through the villages. Ask for a map at the rental place or your guest house.\nYou can also bicycle to Jar Site 1 from Phonsavan. Depending on which map you use, it's 8\u201315\u00a0km away and although the roads have some inclines it's possible to cycle there without being especially fit (maximum 45 min). It's easy to find: the junction off the main road is signposted and from that village it's 2.5\u00a0km. Parking your bike at Jar site 1 costs 2,000 kip on top of the entrance fee. If you arrive before 07:00, the site is deserted and nobody will ask for an entrance or parking fee.\nSee.\nIn Phonsavan, many companies offer tours of the Plain of Jars and around surrounding villages and other sights. See the Phonsavan section for activities and attractions around the Plain of Jars.\nThere are hundreds of jars at the various sites. Some were damaged in the Indochina War, and some during the invasion of the Chinese bandits. They are showing wear and tear caused by tourists climbing on them. Some of the sites have impressive bomb craters and jars split by the force of the bombing.\n is one of the most impressive and the easiest to get to. Entrance fee to Site 1 is 35,000 kip and to and at 30,000 kip each. Take a (motorbike) tour to Jar Sites 2 and 3. However, there are only a few signs, so don't be afraid to ask local people for directions or look for the blue and white painted sign to go to Site 2 or a spray-painted sign on an electric post to find Site 3. There is an easy hike between the two sites suitable for families. The path was cleared of unexploded ordnance in 2007, but visitors are strongly advised to follow the red and white markers.\nOther visitable sites include Jar Sites 16, 23 and 52.\nBuy.\nTypical local products from Phonsavanh and the surrounding area are natural dyes and textiles, each with individual pattern depending on the ethnic group, and basketry, mulberry paper umbrellas, spoons and bangles made from war scrap or Hmong embroidery. Taking antiques home from Laos may be illegal.\nA special drink is Mastake whisky made from Hed Wai, a highly valued mushroom from the pine forests of Xieng Khoung.\nEat.\nDon't bank on eating a meal along the road between Phonsavan and Jar Site 1 as once you leave the outskirts there's only one village. You can buy water and snacks. There is a small cafe at Site 1.\nThe nearest place for food is Phonsavan.\nDrink.\nThere are several salabeers around Phonsavan overlooking small lakes. The best to find are opposite Maly Hotel or on the road to the airport near Nam Ngum market.\nSleep.\nCheck the Phonsavan section for hotel descriptions. Phonsavan has a wide range of hotels from simple guesthouses to more luxurious hotels and even a French boutique hotel on top of a mountain.\nStay safe.\nDuring the Laotian Civil War of 1959\u20131975, a.k.a. the \"Secret War\", the Plain of Jars was in the middle of contested territory that was heavily bombed by the US and heavily mined by both sides. Decades later, there is still a lot of unexploded ordnance (UXO) on the ground in the valley, which continues to kill or maim Laotians. However, the international NGO Mines Advisory Group (MAG) has worked at demining the area continually since 1994, and special attention was paid to the Plain of Jars sites to make them safe for visitors. Visitors today are quite safe so long as they stay within site boundaries that are clearly marked with MAG marker bricks. The white side of the marker indicates the safe side, and the red side indicates an area that has yet to be checked for mines. To learn more about UXO around the Plain of Jars and the ongoing MAG operations, visit their information centre in central Phonsavan.\nGo next.\nThere is a daily (morning) bus to Luang Prabang, with the 8-hour journey costing 95,000 kip (Mar 2020). The many minivans going to Luang Prabang cost around 120,000 kip. There is also a daily bus to Vinh in Vietnam. A night bus to Vientiane, arriving the next morning, costs around 130,000 kip for a VIP bus and 110,000 kip for local bus. See Phonsavan for more details."}
{"id": "27792", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=27792", "title": "Plains (India)", "text": "The Plains of India are the country's heartland. Also called the Hindi Belt, this region contains the large and politically significant states of Uttar Pradesh and Bihar, and the country's capital, New Delhi. With an area of about 400,000 sqkm, the Plains are the home of about half a billion people (about one in every sixteen people alive today), including the densely-populated cities of Delhi, Lucknow, Varanasi and Patna. As a result of this area's continuous habitation for thousands of years and the creativity of the people under various dynasties, some of the most famous sights in the world are on the Indian Plains.\nRegions.\nThe Plains consists of the following four states, listed from west to east:\nIn addition, the Plains consists of two independent cities which are union territories:\nNot only that, but there are regions that cover multiple states:\nCities.\nHere are nine of the most notable cities.\nUnderstand.\nThe Plains, watered by the holy Ganges (\"Gang\u0101\") and its tributaries, are the breadbasket of India. Its western edge is the Punjab plains; its northern edge is the Himalayas, the highest mountains on Earth; its eastern edge is the Ganges Delta of Bengal, the largest river delta in the world; and its southern edge is the plateaus of Bundelkhand and Jharkhand.\nThe region is often called the 'Hindi Belt' (\u0939\u093f\u0902\u0926\u0940 \u092a\u091f\u094d\u091f\u0940 \"Hindi Pa\u1e6d\u1e6d\u012b\"), or the 'Hindi Heartland', as Hindi and Urdu are widely spoken here. It is also called the 'Cow Belt' due to the importance of cattle in this region.\nHistory.\nThe Plains was once part of the Sanskrit belt of \"\u0100ry\u0101varta\" where the Aryans established themselves in India between 2000 and 1500 BCE. The Hinduism as it is known was also developed here.\nAround the 6th century BCE, the small states and chiefdoms in northern India had consolidated into 16 major states or \"mah\u0101janapadas\", 11 of which were on the Gangetic Plain. At the time, the \"Shramana\" (ascetic) movement challenged the Vedic culture and orthodoxy. This gave birth to Buddhism and Jainism, both of which held up renunciation as an ideal and established long-lasting monastic traditions. This led to the development of early Buddhist sites like Kushinagar, Sarnath and Shravasti.\nBy the 3rd century BCE, the Magadha kingdom had annexed or reduced other states and evolved into the Maurya Empire, with the capital in Pataliputra (present-day Patna). Although the first two Mauryan emperors were focused on empire-building and determined management of public life, the third emperor Ashoka renounced any further conquests beyond Kalinga (present-day Odisha and Andhra Pradesh), and focused on the advocacy of Buddhism. This is when the various Ashokan pillars were erected across the Gangetic Plain. However, after Ashoka's death, the empire eventually fractured into numerous small states.\nBy the 3rd century CE, the Magadha region gave birth to another empire under the House of Gupta. Often called India's golden age, the Gupta period saw the revival of both Hinduism and Sanskrit in India. This is when Indian astronomy, medicine and mathematics made significant advances, as well as the Sanskrit literature.\nThe decline of the Guptas in the 6th century marked the early Middle Ages in India, marked by various regional kingdoms and cultural diversity. The Harsha of Kanyakubja (present-day Kannauj) and his successors attempted to expand beyond the Indian Plains, but got defeated in the conflicts with other regional rulers. At that time, Kanyakubja as well as Varanasi emerged as religious and commercial centres, supported by an expanding temple economy and patronage networks.\nBy the 10th century, the nomads of Central Asia repeatedly overran northwestern India. Gradually the raiders started staying as rulers, and soon the entire India Plains were ruled by Muslims, including the Sultans of Delhi, and later the Mughals and the Afghans. The peace maintained by the Mughals during much of the 17th century led to the region's economic expansion, resulting in greater patronage of painting, literary forms, textiles and architecture. This is when various monuments were produced in Agra and Delhi, including the Taj Mahal and the Red Fort. This is also when the region developed into the Hindi Belt with Hindu and Urdu literatures. The Grand Trunk Road from Sonargaon to Kabul was also developed significantly during that period.\nFounded by Guru Nanak in the 15th century, Sikhism flourished in Punjab during the Mughal rule. This led to the conflict between the Sikhs and the Mughals. The empire got weakened after the death of Aurangzeb in 1707. This is when the regional powers asserted political control, including the Sikhs of Punjab, the Nawabs of Awadh and the Nawabs of Bengal. The British East India Company (EIC) took advantage of this situation and eventually took control over much of the Indian Plains by the early 19th century.\nThe mid-19th century saw the growth of India's education as well as technology under the British rule. However, by 1857, the growing disaffection with the British led to significant uprisings in cities like Delhi, Jhansi, Kanpur, Lucknow and Meerut. Although the rebellion was suppressed in 1858, the subsequent resistance to British colonialism led to India's independence in 1947. However, the same year, the country was partitioned to form the Muslim-majority Pakistan, which proved to be a major disaster. Several million people were uprooted, Muslims migrated from their homes in India to live in Pakistan, with Hindus and Sikhs moving the other way. This overpopulated the Indian Plains, especially Punjab and Delhi.\nThe decades after independence saw various development in the Plains. In 1966, Punjab was partitioned to form Haryana and Himachal Pradesh. At the same year, the region experienced the Green Revolution, positioning it as India's breadbasket.\nThe Plains experienced the Khalistan insurgency in the 1980s, which was later suppressed. The region also saw statehood movements for Jharkhand and Uttarakhand in the 1990s, which led to the formation of both states in 2000.\nTalk.\nHindi and Urdu are widely spoken in almost all of the states as this is the home of the Hindi belt, with the exception of Punjab, where Punjabi dominates. Maithili language is spoken in Bihar, alongside Hindi. When you speak in Hindi everyone will understand usually.\nAs in the rest of India, English is a very commonly learned second and third language especially in Delhi, Punjab and UP.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nAll major cities in the area have airports. Delhi's Indira Gandhi International Airport is large, and likely to be the point of entry for most foreign visitors to this region. There are also international airports at Lucknow, Amritsar and Chandigarh. Domestic flights from other parts of India are also commonplace in the smaller tier-2, tier-3 cities.\nBy train and bus.\nLong-distance trains travel from all other parts of India to this region. The same is true of long-distance bus lines.\nGet around.\nThere are numerous train connections between the major cities in this region. Domestic flights for longer distances are also possible. Buses also traverse this area.\nSee.\nThe Plains contain some of the most famous sights in the world. Agra is the home of the Taj Mahal; near Agra is the former royal town of Fatehpur Sikri, which is made out of red sandstone. The Ganges at Varanasi is both a sight and an experience \u2014 the crowds of people ritually bathing in the river, the funeral pyres near its banks, the splendid ghats. There are quite a few other places along the Ganges, Yamuna and other rivers sacred to Hindus that are worth visiting. The towns of Bodh Gaya and Sarnath are two of the wellsprings of Buddhism; Bodh Gaya in particular has impressive temples. Amritsar's pride and joy is the Golden Temple, the world headquarters of the Sikh religion. And of course there is the metropolis of Delhi, which has numerous sights including the Jama Masjid and Qutb Minar, two of the best known examples of Islamic architecture in the country, and the Red Fort.\nEat.\nThe plains of North India offer some of the most diverse and flavourful cuisines in the country, shaped by fertile farmland, long culinary traditions, and a mix of Mughal, Punjabi, Awadhi, and regional influences. Food here is generally rich, wheat\u2011based, and aromatic, with an emphasis on breads, slow\u2011cooked curries, and tandoor\u2011style cooking.\nPunjab and Haryana are known for hearty, dairy\u2011rich meals. Travellers will find \"parathas\", \"sarson ka saag\", \"makki ki roti\", and a wide range of tandoori dishes. Roadside \"dhabas\" are a major part of the food culture, serving simple but satisfying meals such as dal, roti, and fresh buttermilk.\nIn Delhi, the cuisine reflects centuries of Mughal influence alongside modern street\u2011food traditions. Popular dishes include butter chicken\", \"kebabs\", \"biryani, and an extensive variety of snacks such as chaat\", \"golgappas\", and \"aloo tikki. The city\u2019s old quarters are especially known for their sweets, breads, and grilled meats.\nUttar Pradesh offers several distinct regional cuisines. The Awadhi food of Lucknow is famous for its slow\u2011cooked dishes like galouti kebabs\", \"nihari\", and \"dum biryani\". In the eastern plains, travellers will find lighter vegetarian meals, lentils, rice, and seasonal vegetables. Cities like Varanasi are known for snacks such as kachori sabzi\", \"malaiyo\", and a wide range of sweets.\nAcross the plains, wheat breads such as roti\", \"naan\", and \"paratha are staples, and dairy products\u2014especially ghee, curd, and paneer\u2014feature prominently. Street food is widely available and varies from city to city, offering travellers an easy way to sample local flavours.\nDrink.\nThe plains of North India offer a wide range of traditional drinks, from refreshing summer coolers to rich dairy\u2011based beverages. Lassi is one of the most popular drinks across Punjab, Haryana, and Uttar Pradesh\u2014served sweet, salted, or flavoured with fruit. In Punjab, it is often thick and creamy, sometimes topped with a layer of malai. Chaas (buttermilk) is another common drink, especially in rural areas, and is valued for its cooling properties during hot summers.\nIn the cities of the plains, especially Delhi and Lucknow, travellers will find a variety of street\u2011side drinks such as shikanji (spiced lemonade), jaljeera, and seasonal fruit juices. During the summer months, sugarcane juice is widely available and freshly pressed at roadside stalls. In winter, the region offers warm drinks like masala chai, which is served throughout the day in homes, markets, and railway stations.\nSeveral regions also have their own traditional beverages. Uttar Pradesh is known for thandai, a milk\u2011based drink flavoured with nuts and spices, especially popular during festivals. In the eastern Gangetic plains, travellers may encounter sattu drink, made from roasted gram flour mixed with water, salt, or jaggery. Alcohol is available in most cities, with beer and Indian spirits commonly served in licensed establishments, though availability may vary by state regulations (for example, Bihar is a dry state).\nStay safe.\nThe Plains have a higher crime rate than the West. Pickpockets are very common in urban areas. Carry your cash, passport and cards in a secure money belt, with only enough cash for a few hours in your wallet or another accessible place. However, if you can use UPI (India's digital payment system) this is not as much of a concern.\nThe region also has a terrible reputation for crime and banditry (or \"dacoity\", to use the Indian word)."}
{"id": "27793", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=27793", "title": "North Costa Rica", "text": "North Costa Rica, also known as \"\"Regi\u00f3n Norte\" or \"Huetar Norte\"\" (in Spanish) is a region located within Costa Rica and borders in the north to Rio San Juan Region of Nicaragua.\nUnderstand.\nThis region is known for Arenal Volcano, but it has other gems that are not-so-hidden such as Lake Arenal and the Los Angeles Cloud Forest.\nArenal is the world's 3rd most active volcano and can be heard rumbling several times an hour. The lava flow changes frequently, but even without seeing the lava the mountain is impressive in size and shape.\nThe Los Angeles Cloud Forest is not as visited as the Cloud Forests in Monteverde, but the wildlife is abundant and the locals are extremely friendly.\nThe city San Ramon is a moderately sized town that really only has one tourist attraction, the local church or cathedral. The church is large and in a state of repair but it is next to the town's central park which follows Japanese influence in its design. The north-eastern parts of this region are dominated by mono-cultural exploitation of sugar cane, citrus trees and pineapple farms.\nGet in.\nThe best way to get to the North Zone is by car. There are also local buses that travel San Jos\u00e9 and San Ramon, but the car offers the most flexibility to stop and experience the local culture on your trip to the North.\nFrom Nicaragua you can cross the border by boat at San Carlos (Nicaragua)/Los Chiles, but it is not possible to take a car with you. From Los Chiles there are regular buses to Ciudad Quesada. A much faster overland border crossing is now open via a bridge over the Rio San Juan. The border post is at Las Tablillas 6 km from Los Chiles; it is open 08:00-16:00.\nThe same buses that come from San Jos\u00e9 and Ciudad Quesada serve the remote border post for around \u20a1500 from Los Chiles. A land exit tax of ~US$7.00 is payable when leaving Costa Rica and an arrival tax of ~US$12.00 on arrival in Nicaragua, only payable in US dollars or c\u00f3rdobas (Sept 2017).\nBuses from San Jos\u00e9 to Los Chiles leave each morning and afternoon at around 05:30 and 15:00 and take about 5 hr.\nGet around.\nFrequent buses travel the area. Bus times are considerably unreliable and infrequent in La Fortuna, probably due to forcing tourists into taking a taxi. Otherwise, between the larger towns and cities there are buses every 1-2 hr.\nSleep.\nAround R\u00edo Celeste there are many inexpensive guest houses available. Checkout GoogleMaps or AirBnB to find them."}
